.SH NAME
X509V3_EXT_nconf, X509V3_EXT_nconf_nid \- functions to
create X.509 certificate extensions based on OpenSSL configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 1
\& #include <openssl/x509v3.h>
\&
\& X509_EXTENSION *X509V3_EXT_nconf_nid(CONF *conf, X509V3_CTX *ctx, int ext_nid,
\&     const char *value);
\& X509_EXTENSION *X509V3_EXT_nconf(CONF *conf, X509V3_CTX *ctx, const char *name,
\&     const char *value);
.Ve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
\&\fBX509V3_EXT_nconf_nid()\fR and \fBX509V3_EXT_nconf()\fR create a requested X.509
extension identified either by \fIname\fR or \fIext_nid\fR correspondingly using
\&\fIvalue\fR contains the string to encode.
.PP
\&\fIconf\fR the configuration information where additional data
will be read from. \fIconf\fR will typically come from a config
file.
.PP
In case when \fIctx\fR value is null, the default one is used.
.SH NOTES
.IX Header "NOTES"
Normally these functions should not be called directly. For many extensions
they will not produce the expected results without a suitably configured
context (\fIconf\fR and \fIctx\fR arguments) that specifies the target object, issuer
data etc.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
.IX Header "RETURN VALUES"
\&\fBX509V3_EXT_nconf()\fR and \fBX509V3_EXT_nconf_nid()\fR return an \fBX509_EXTENSION\fR
object on success and NULL on failure.
